      "ts": "2026-02-15T12:40:33.861-08:00",
      "player": "Gem25P Coach",
      "type": "llm_response",
      "reasoning": "THINK:\n1.  **Analyze the Situation:** I've just played a Forest. This has triggered two landfall abilities:\n    *   Aesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait: \"Whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control, you may draw a card.\"\n    *   Avenger of Zendikar: \"Whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control, you may put a +1/+1 counter on each Plant creature you control.\"\n    *   Khalni Heart Expedition: \"Whenever a land enters the battlefield under your control, you may put a quest counter on Khalni Heart Expedition.\"\n\n2.  **Review the Choices:** The `get_action_choices` output confirms this. It's asking me to \"Select triggered abilities to put on the stack\". The choices presented are:\n    *   `index: 0, name: \"Avenger of Zendikar\"`\n    *   `index: 1, name: \"Aesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait\"`\n    *   `index: 2, name: \"Khalni Heart Expedition\"`\n\n3.  **Strategic Stacking (S.N.A.P.):** The order in which I put these triggers on the stack matters. The last one I choose (`choose_action`) will be the first one to resolve. My goal is to maximize my advantage *this turn*.\n\n4.  **Evaluate Trigger Order:**\n    *   **Option A (Draw First):** Stack Aesi's trigger last. This means `[Khalni Heart, Avenger, Aesi]`. Aesi resolves first, I draw a card. Then Avenger resolves, pumping my plants. Then Khalni Heart gets a counter. The benefit here is seeing a new card before deciding anything else. The new card might be an instant I want to use or another land I can play (thanks to Aesi's other ability).\n    *   **Option B (Pump First):** Stack Avenger's trigger last. This means `[Khalni Heart, Aesi, Avenger]`. Avenger resolves first, my plants get bigger. Then Aesi resolves, I draw. Then Khalni Heart gets a counter. This is less optimal because drawing the card *after* the pump provides less information.\n    *   **Option C (Ramp First):** Stack Khalni Heart's trigger last. This means `[Aesi, Avenger, Khalni Heart]`. The Expedition gets a counter first. This is the least impactful resolution order. It doesn't give me information (like drawing) or immediate board presence improvement (like pumping for a potential combat).\n\n5.  **Choose the Best Order:** Drawing a card with Aesi first is almost always correct. It gives me the most information and potentially unlocks more plays this turn. After drawing, pumping the plants with Avenger of Zendikar is the next best thing to resolve. Finally, the Khalni Heart Expedition counter will resolve. So, the resolution order I want is: Aesi -> Avenger -> Khalni Heart.\n\n6.  **Translate to Action:** To get that resolution order, I need to put them on the stack in the *reverse* order. So I will choose them in this sequence:\n    1.  Khalni Heart Expedition (`index: 2`)\n    2.  Avenger of Zendikar (`index: 0`)\n    3.  Aesi, Tyrant of Gyre Strait (`index: 1`)\n\n7.  **Construct the Action:** I will make three `choose_action` calls in a row with the appropriate indices. The first call will be `choose_action(index=2)`.Okay, team, landfall! Playing that Forest triggered three of our abilities at once: Aesi lets us draw a card, Avenger of Zendikar pumps our Plant tokens, and Khalni Heart Expedition gets a quest counter.\n\nWe get to choose the order these happen in, and that's a key strategic decision. Here's the game plan:\n\n1.  We want to draw a card with Aesi *first*. This gives us more information and could even give us another land to play this turn.\n2.  Then, we'll have the Avenger's ability resolve to put +1/+1 counters on all our Plant tokens, making our army even bigger.\n3.  Finally, we'll get the counter on Khalni Heart Expedition.\n\nTo make them happen in that order, we have to put them on the stack in the *reverse* order. So, first up is the Khalni Heart Expedition. Let's do it",
