      "player": "Gem25P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 11
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Playing Mystic Monastery (enters tapped) instead of Plains (enters untapped) on turn 1 means DSV3 Stoic cannot cast Faithless Looting on turn 2 if they draw a non-tapped land, since Mystic Monastery won't produce red until next turn anyway. However, more importantly, Plains entering untapped would leave mana available, and on turn 2, playing Mystic Monastery tapped while having Plains untapped would allow casting Faithless Looting turn 2. With Mystic Monastery first, turn 2 they play Plains untapped and can still cast Faithless Looting - so the sequencing is actually similar. The real issue is that Plains doesn't produce red for Faithless Looting. Mystic Monastery produces U/R/W, so playing it turn 1 (tapped) means it's ready turn 2 for Faithless Looting if they play any other land. Playing Plains first means they'd need another red source for Faithless Looting. Actually, Mystic Monastery is likely the correct play here since it enables Faithless Looting on turn 2 with any untapped land. However, Prairie Stream checks for two basic lands - playing Plains first would help toward that. With only one basic (Plains) in hand, Prairie Stream will enter tapped regardless. So Mystic Monastery turn 1 is reasonable for enabling turn 2 Faithless Looting.",
      "actionTaken": "Played Mystic Monastery (enters tapped, produces U/R/W)",
      "betterLine": "This is actually reasonable - Mystic Monastery enables Faithless Looting on turn 2. No clear better line.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 9,
      "player": "DSV3 Stoic",
      "decisionIndex": 16
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With Growth Spiral in hand (an instant that draws a card and puts a land into play), the optimal turn 2 play is to play Island untapped, then cast Growth Spiral using Command Tower + Island, draw a card, and potentially put Woodland Stream or Memorial to Genius onto the battlefield as the extra land drop. This would net an extra card and an extra land compared to just playing Woodland Stream tapped. By playing Woodland Stream (which enters tapped), the player has no untapped mana to cast Growth Spiral this turn, wasting a full turn of development.",
      "actionTaken": "Played Woodland Stream (enters tapped), leaving no untapped lands to cast Growth Spiral.",
      "betterLine": "Play Island untapped, then cast Growth Spiral using Command Tower + Island to draw a card and potentially put an additional land (like Woodland Stream or Memorial to Genius) onto the battlefield.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 21,
      "player": "Gem25P Coach",
      "decisionIndex": 22
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Devil's Play has flashback ({X}{R}{R}{R}), making it the ideal card to discard to Faithless Looting since it retains full value from the graveyard. This is a core synergy of Faithless Looting - discarding cards with flashback or graveyard value. Boros Guildgate, while a tapped land, is permanently lost when discarded.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Boros Guildgate as first discard to Faithless Looting",
      "betterLine": "Discard Devil's Play as one of the two cards, since it has flashback and can be cast from the graveyard later, preserving its full value while getting maximum benefit from Faithless Looting's draw-discard effect.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 26,
      "player": "DSV3 Stoic",
      "decisionIndex": 30
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "DSV3 Stoic is discarding Azorius Chancery to Faithless Looting. While Azorius Chancery enters tapped and bounces a land, it's a dual-mana producing land that would be very valuable in a deck that needs W, U, and R mana. Looking at their hand after the first discard (which we don't see but this is the second pick), they still have Boros Guildgate, Prairie Stream, and Island as land options. However, Azorius Chancery produces both W and U mana every turn - it's effectively a ramp card. Meanwhile, cards like Magmaquake and Devil's Play are X-spells that are very expensive and less useful in the early game. Discarding Magmaquake (which requires a lot of mana to be effective and is situational) or Devil's Play (which has flashback so it can be cast from the graveyard anyway) would be significantly better choices. Devil's Play specifically has flashback {X}{R}{R}{R}, meaning discarding it actually enables casting it later from the graveyard - it's practically designed to be discarded.",
      "actionTaken": "Discarded Azorius Chancery, a dual-mana-producing land, to Faithless Looting.",
